---
title: 'A Lightweight ATS for Startups: Pipeline Stages, Boards, Notes and Email Templates'
publishedAt: 2026-10-21
type: companies
---
**Quick answer:** GetHired includes a lightweight applicant tracking system (ATS) built for startups. Each job has a pipeline with stages you can customise, a list and a drag-and-drop board, bulk moves, internal notes and ratings, an activity timeline, email templates with your scheduling link, applicant notifications and a hiring funnel. It's free to start and needs no setup beyond posting a job.

Most early-stage teams run hiring from a spreadsheet next to their job board. It works for the first role. By the third, nobody knows who emailed which candidate, which interviews are pending, or why someone was turned down.

Full ATS products such as Greenhouse, Lever or Workday solve this for large companies, but they take weeks to set up. (Job seekers can read [what each of those ATS products looks like from the other side](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/greenhouse-vs-workday-vs-lever-vs-workable-what-job-seekers-should-know-about-each-ats).) GetHired gives you the parts a small team actually uses.

## Pipeline stages

Every company starts with sensible default stages:

**Applied → Screening → Interview → Offer → Hired**, plus **Rejected** and **On hold**.

Rename them, reorder them, archive the ones you don't use and add your own (up to 12), such as "Take-home" or "Founder chat". Each stage maps to a simple status the candidate sees, so applicants always know where they stand without seeing your internal stage names.

Set them up in **Settings → Pipeline stages**.

## List and board views

Each job's applications page has two views:

- **List**: a sortable, filterable table. Sort by [AI fit score](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/ai-applicant-screening-how-to-sort-200-applicants-by-fit-without-reading-them-all), filter by stage, recommendation or answers to your screening questions.
- **Board**: one column per stage. Each card shows the candidate's name, fit score, days in the stage, note count and AI interview status. Drag a card to move it. It works with a keyboard too, and on a phone the board becomes a stage picker with a single list.

## Bulk moves with optional emails

Select up to 50 applicants and move them to a stage in one go. When the move changes what the candidate sees (for example to Interview or Rejected), GetHired offers to email them using the matching template. You see a preview before anything is sent, and sending is always optional.

## Notes and ratings

Leave notes on any application or talent-pool candidate, in markdown, for your team. A note can carry a rating: strong no, no, yes or strong yes. The application page shows each rating and the average, so a debrief takes five minutes instead of a meeting.

Notes are internal. Candidates never see them, and they're never included in emails.

## An activity timeline for every application

Every stage move, email, note, AI screening, interview and outreach is recorded on the application's timeline, along with who did it. When a cofounder asks "did we ever get back to this person?", the answer is one click away.

## Email templates and scheduling links

Each company has editable templates for:

- rejection;
- moving forward;
- interview invite;
- offer;
- custom messages.

Templates support variables such as `candidate_first_name`, `job_title`, `company_name`, `scheduling_link` and `sender_name`. Add your Calendly, Cal.com or Google appointment link in **Settings → Scheduling**, and interview invites include it automatically, so candidates book a slot without the back-and-forth.

Emails are sent from GetHired with replies going to your company's contact email.

## Notifications that don't flood your inbox

Choose how you hear about new applicants:

- **Daily digest** (the default): one email each morning with new applicants per job and their fit scores.
- **Instant**: an email per application, throttled to one per job every 15 minutes.
- **Off.**

A weekly email also lists new matched candidates for each active job.

## The hiring funnel

Each job shows a funnel: how many candidates reached each stage, conversion between stages, median days in each stage, where candidates came from (applied, outreach or the agent) and time to hire. The dashboard adds a **needs attention** list of applications that have sat in Applied for more than three days with nothing happening.

## Talent lists

Group saved candidates into named lists like "Backend shortlist". Adding a talent-pool candidate to a job's pipeline sends them an [outreach invite](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/how-to-find-and-contact-candidates-who-want-to-be-found-consent-based-outreach) rather than creating an application on their behalf.

## Or just ask the agent

Every pipeline action is also available through the [recruiter agent](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/meet-the-gethired-recruiter-agent-hire-tech-talent-from-one-chat): "Move everyone I rated strong yes to Interview and send them our invite." It shows you the recipients, stage and email before anything happens.

## A sensible daily cap

To protect your sender reputation (and candidates' inboxes), bulk emails are limited to 50 recipients per action and a daily total per company. A move that would exceed the daily cap is refused before anything changes.

[Start hiring free](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/auth/sign-up?company=true) and your first job comes with a ready-made pipeline.
